#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overview {#overview}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Partial Differential Equation (PDE) solver presented here is part of our"
" Quantum Innovative Computing Kit (QUICK) platform (QUICK-PDE), and is "
"packaged as a Quantum Function. With the QUICK-PDE function, you can solve "
"domain-specific partial differential equations on IBM Quantum QPUs. This "
"function is based on the algorithm described in [ColibriTD's H-DES "
"description paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01130). This algorithm can "
"solve complex multi-physics problems, starting with Computational Fluid "
"Dynamics (CFD) and Materials Deformation (MD), and now including "
"quantitative finance with the Black-Scholes equation for options on one to "
"three assets. Other use cases are coming soon.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To tackle the differential equations, the trial solutions are encoded as "
"linear combinations of orthogonal functions (typically Chebyshev "
"polynomials, and more specifically up to $2^n$ of them where $n$ is the "
"number of qubits encoding your function), parametrized by the angles of a "
"Variable Quantum Circuit (VQC). The ansatz generates a state encoding the "
"function, which is evaluated by observables whose combinations allow for "
"evaluating the function at all points. You can then evaluate the loss "
"function in which the differential equations are encoded, and fine-tune the "
"angles in a hybrid loop, as shown in the following. The trial solutions get "
"gradually closer to the actual solutions until you reach a satisfactory "
"result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Workflow of the QUICK-PDE function](/docs/images/guides/colibritd-"
"equation-solver/diagram.svg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In addition to this hybrid loop, you can also chain together different "
"optimizers. This is useful when you want a global optimizer to find a good "
"set of angles, and then a more fine-tuned optimizer to follow a gradient to "
"the best set of neighboring angles. In the case of computational fluid "
"dynamics (CFD), the default optimization sequence produces the best results "
"- but in the case of material deformation (MD), while the default provides "
"good results, you can configure it further for problem-specific benefits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note that for each variable of the function, you specify the number of "
"qubits (which you can experiment with). By stacking 10 identical circuits "
"and evaluating the 10 identical observables on different qubits throughout "
"one big circuit, you can noise-mitigate within the CMA optimization process "
"or parallelize the PSR computations, relying on the noise learner method, "
"and significantly reducing the number of shots needed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Computational fluid dynamics {#computational-fluid-dynamics}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The inviscid Burgers' equation** models flowing non-viscous fluids as "
"follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} + u\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = 0$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$u$ represents the fluid speed field. This use-case has a temporal boundary "
"condition: you can select the initial condition and then allow the system to"
" relax. Currently, the only accepted initial conditions are linear "
"functions: $ax + b$. The analytical solution is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$u(t, x) = \\frac{ax + b}{at + 1}$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The pressureless Euler equations** model compressible inviscid fluid flow "
"with damping as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial t} + u\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} + "
"g\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = 0$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$u\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial t} + g\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} + "
"u^2\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial x} + 2gu\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} + "
"\\frac{\\mu}{(1+t)^{\\lambda}} g u = 0$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$g$ represents the density field, $u$ the velocity field and $\\mu$ a "
"damping coefficient. In our formulation, we pose $\\lambda = 1$, so it will "
"not be used as a parameter in the following. This use-case has temporal "
"boundary conditions: $g(0, x) = e^{-x}$ and $u(0, x) = x$. The analytical "
"solution is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$g(t, x) = \\frac{1-\\mu}{(1+t)^{1-\\mu} - \\mu} "
"\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{(\\mu-1)\\, x}{(1+t)^{1-\\mu} - \\mu}\\right)$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$u(t, x) = \\frac{(1-\\mu)\\, x}{\\left((1+t)^{1-\\mu} - \\mu\\right)(1+t)^{\\mu}}$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The arguments for CFD's differential equations are on a fixed grid, as "
"follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$t$ is between 0 and 0.95 with 41 sample points. $x$ is between 0 and 0.95 "
"with 41 sample points.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Material deformation {#material-deformation}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This use case focuses on **hypoelastic deformation with the one-dimensional "
"tensile test**, in which a bar fixed in space is pulled at its other "
"extremity. We describe the problem as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$u' - \\frac{\\sigma}{3K} - "
"\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\epsilon_0\\left(\\frac{\\sigma'}{\\sigma_0\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^n"
" = 0$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$\\sigma' - b = 0$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$K$ represents the bulk modulus of the material being stretched, $n$ the "
"exponent of a power law, $b$ the force per unit mass, $\\epsilon_0$ the "
"proportional stress limit, $\\sigma_0$ the proportional strain limit, $u$ "
"the stress function, and $\\sigma$ the strain function. The analytical "
"solution is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$\\sigma(x) = \\sigma_0 - bx$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$u(x) = -\\frac{3^{-(3+n)/2}}{2bK(1+n)\\,\\sigma_0^{n}}\\Biggl[3^{(1+n)/2}b^2\\sigma_0^n(1+n)x^2 - 2\\cdot 3^{(1+n)/2}b\\sigma_0^n(1+n)\\sigma_0 x - 12\\epsilon_0 K\\sigma_0^{1+n}$\n"
"$- 12b\\epsilon_0 K\\sigma_0^n x\\left(\\frac{-bx+\\sigma_0}{\\sigma_0}\\right)^n + 12\\epsilon_0 K\\sigma_0^{n+1}\\left(\\frac{-bx+\\sigma_0}{\\sigma_0}\\right)^n - 12\\epsilon_0 K \\sigma_0^{1+n}\\Biggr]$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $\\sigma_0 = g(0)$ is the boundary condition on the strain at $x=0$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The considered bar is of unitary length. This use-case has a boundary "
"condition for surface stress $t$, or the amount of work needed to stretch "
"the bar.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The arguments for MD's differential equations are on a fixed grid, as "
"follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$x$ is between 0 and 1 with 30 sample points.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Quantitative finance {#quantitative-finance}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**The Black-Scholes equation** gives the price $C$ of a European call option"
" as a function of the time $t$ and of the price of the underlying asset or "
"assets. We pose a zero interest rate, no dividends and a maturity $T = 1$. "
"This use case is available for one, two and three assets.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For a single asset, the equation reads:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$\\frac{\\partial C}{\\partial t} + \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2} S^2 "
"\\frac{\\partial^2 C}{\\partial S^2} = 0$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $\\sigma$ is the volatility of the asset and $S$ its price. The "
"boundary conditions are given by the analytical solution. It fixes the price"
" at the final time $t = 0.9$ (a residual maturity of 0.1) and at the edges "
"$S = 1$ and $S = 2$ of the price domain, and the solver recovers the price "
"at the earlier times. The analytical solution is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$C(t, S) = S\\,\\Phi(d_1) - K\\,\\Phi(d_2), \\qquad d_{1,2} = "
"\\frac{\\ln(S/K) \\pm \\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}(T-t)}{\\sigma\\sqrt{T-t}}$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $K$ is the strike of the option and $\\Phi$ is the cumulative "
"distribution function of the standard normal distribution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For **baskets of two and three assets**, the option is written on the "
"geometric average $G = \\prod_{i=1}^{n} S_i^{w_i}$ of the $n$ asset prices, "
"where the weights $w_i$ are strictly positive and sum to 1:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$\\frac{\\partial C}{\\partial t} + \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\frac{\\sigma_i^2}{2} "
"S_i^2 \\frac{\\partial^2 C}{\\partial S_i^2} + \\sum_{i<j} "
"\\rho_{ij}\\,\\sigma_i\\sigma_j\\, S_i S_j \\frac{\\partial^2 C}{\\partial "
"S_i \\partial S_j} = 0$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $\\sigma_i$ is the volatility of asset $i$ and $\\rho_{ij}$ the "
"correlation between assets $i$ and $j$. The boundary conditions are given by"
" the analytical solution on the final time and on the edges of the price "
"domain. With $\\tau = T - t$, the analytical solution is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$C = G\\,e^{-q\\tau}\\,\\Phi(d_1) - K\\,\\Phi(d_2), \\qquad d_1 = "
"\\frac{\\ln(G/K) + \\left(\\frac{\\sigma_G^2}{2} - "
"q\\right)\\tau}{\\sigma_G\\sqrt{\\tau}}, \\qquad d_2 = d_1 - "
"\\sigma_G\\sqrt{\\tau}$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$\\sigma_G^2 = \\sum_{i} w_i^2\\sigma_i^2 + 2\\sum_{i<j} \\rho_{ij}\\, w_i "
"w_j\\, \\sigma_i\\sigma_j, \\qquad q = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\sum_i "
"w_i\\sigma_i^2 - \\sigma_G^2\\right)$,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "and the one-asset solution is recovered for $n = 1$ and $w_1 = 1$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The physical parameters of the use case are the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Parameter                             | Description                                                                                                   | Range                                                          | Business range                                                                  |\n"
"| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n"
"| `sigma`, `sigma1`, `sigma2`, `sigma3` | Volatility of each asset                                                                                      | 0.05 to 1                                                      | 0.10 to 0.40                                                                    |\n"
"| `K`                                   | Strike of the option                                                                                          | 1 to 2                                                         | 1.2 to 1.8 (80% to 120% of a spot price at the center of the domain, $S = 1.5$) |\n"
"| `w1` (basket of 2 assets)             | Weight of the first asset. The weight of the second asset is deduced: $w_2 = 1 - w_1$                         | Each weight is strictly positive (so $0 < w_1 < 1$)            | Each weight from 0.2 to 0.8                                                     |\n"
"| `w1`, `w2` (basket of 3 assets)       | Weights of the first and of the second asset. The weight of the third asset is deduced: $w_3 = 1 - w_1 - w_2$ | Each weight is strictly positive (so $w_1 + w_2 < 1$)          | Each of the three weights from 0.2 to 0.8                                       |\n"
"| `rho`, `rho12`, `rho13`, `rho23`      | Correlation between two assets                                                                                | 0 to 1 (the correlation matrix must be positive semi-definite) | 0.1 to 0.8                                                                      |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The range column gives the values accepted by the use case. The business "
"range gives the values commonly met in practice: volatilities of equity "
"indices, large capitalizations and currencies, strikes close to the spot "
"price, moderate to high correlations between assets, and a residual maturity"
" $T - t$ between 0.3 and 1 year.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The arguments for the Black-Scholes differential equations are on a fixed "
"domain, as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$t$ is between 0 and 0.9. The variable $x_i$ of each asset is between 0 and "
"1, and the price of the asset is $S_i = 1 + x_i$, so between 1 and 2.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansatz {#ansatz}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The variable quantum circuit that encodes each function is selected with the"
" `ansatz` argument:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`HEA` is a hardware-efficient ansatz measured with a global observable. It "
"is the most general choice.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`BPM-LE` (Barren Plateau Mitigation, Low Expressivity) is an ansatz designed"
" to avoid barren plateaus, measured with local observables. It has few "
"parameters and shallow circuits, which keeps the cost of the optimization "
"low, including the cost of parameter-shift gradients.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`BPM-HE` (Barren Plateau Mitigation, High Expressivity) follows the same "
"construction with more parameters per circuit, for functions that the low-"
"expressivity version cannot represent accurately enough.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The number of qubits per variable and the depth of the circuit remain "
"controlled by the `nb_qubits` and `depth` arguments.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Optimizer chain and error metric {#optimizer-chain-and-error-metric}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The optimizers are chained as a list of stages with the `optimizers` "
"argument. Each stage defines its own method, its maximum number of "
"iterations and its own number of shots, so that cheap and noisy evaluations "
"explore the landscape first, and the number of shots increases as the "
"optimization converges. A typical chain is made of the following pieces:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"One or more `CMAES` stages (global and gradient-free), which identify the "
"right region of the space of solutions. Each stage has its own `shots`, "
"`sigma0`, and `popsize` (population size, that is the number of candidates "
"evaluated at each generation). The `sigma0` argument is the initial standard"
" deviation of the search distribution of CMA-ES, that is, its initial step "
"size: a large value widely explores the space of the circuit parameters, "
"while a small value searches locally around the best candidate found so far."
" Decreasing `sigma0` from one stage to the next therefore moves from "
"exploration to refinement.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A `LS-TRF` stage (Least Squares - Trust Region Reflective), which explores "
"this region to refine the final angles. Without `psr`, `LS-TRF` estimates "
"the Jacobian of the cost function with finite differences. With `psr=True`, "
"the Jacobian is computed on the QPU exactly, with the parameter-shift rule "
"(PSR). The PSR increases the computation time since it requires additional "
"circuits, but it allows a better precision than the finite differences.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The error metric used on this page is the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) "
"between the solution and the reference solution, over the sampled points. "
"The function also reports the mean squared error (MSE) and the maximum "
"absolute deviation, which are the d2 and d1 metrics of Section 2.6 of [the "
"H-DES paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01130).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Plan sizes {#plan-sizes}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The QUICK-PDE function is available in three plan sizes: Small, Medium, and "
"Large. The following table gives the limits and the services of each size.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Size | Mode         | Iterations max (all stages)     | Shots max per job | Max qubits                    | Compute profile (vCPU x RAM Go) |\n"
"| ---- | ------------ | ------------------------------- | ----------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------- |\n"
"| S    | Job mode     | 25                              | 10,000            | 15                            | 8x32                            |\n"
"| M    | Job mode     | 50                              | 50,000            | 35                            | 16x128              |\n"
"| L    | Session mode | Unlimited (up to session limit) | 100,000           | Unlimited (up to QPU limits)  | 48x768                          |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The maximum number of iterations applies to the sum of the iterations of all"
" the stages of the optimizer chain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Function sizes {#function-sizes}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The function runs with a compute size, `\"S\"`, `\"M\"`, or `\"L\"`, which "
"you set with the `function_size` argument of `run`. The size that your "
"problem needs follows the limits of the plans:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The mode (the session mode requires the size `\"L\"`)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The total number of iterations, which is the sum of the `max_iter` of all "
"the stages of the `optimizers` chain\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The maximum number of shots per job, which is the largest `shot` of the "
"stages (more than 100,000 shots per job is not supported)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The maximum number of qubits, which is the number of qubits of the largest "
"function\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The size is the smallest one for which all these values stay within its "
"limits. The onboarding and the support depend on your plan and are not part "
"of the calculation. A problem above the limits of the size `\"M\"` is sized "
"`\"L\"`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If the size you pass is smaller than the size that the problem needs, the "
"job fails with the error code 4600. If it is larger, the job runs and "
"reports an event with the error code 4601 to tell you that a smaller size "
"can be used.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following function calculates the size of a problem from the same "
"arguments as `run`, without submitting anything. When `optimizers` or "
"`nb_qubits` are omitted, the default values of the use case are used, as in "
"`run`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For example, a basket of three assets with a chain of three stages needs a "
"total of 40 iterations and up to 20,000 shots, so it fits size `\"M\"`:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Then pass the size to `run`:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Benchmarks {#benchmarks}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following tables present statistics on various runs of the function. The"
" first table gives the results of the current version of the function. The "
"second table, at the end of this section, only keeps the results of the "
"previous version for reference.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Current version {#current-version}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Example                                    | Total number of qubits | Ansatz   | RMSE                       | Classical compute time (min) | QPU time (min) |\n"
"| ------------------------------------------ | ---------------------- | -------- | -------------------------- | ---------------------------- | -------------- |\n"
"| Inviscid Burgers' equation                 | 4                      | `BPM-LE` | 0.0147                     | ~1                           | 11             |\n"
"| Pressureless Euler's equations             | 8                      | `HEA`    | 0.0193                     | ~1.5                         | 10             |\n"
"| Hypoelastic 1D tensile test                | 6                      | `HEA`    | 0.0296                     | ~1                           | 10             |\n"
"| Black-Scholes equation, 1 asset            | 6                      | `BPM-LE` | 0.0084                     | ~0.3                         | 5.0            |\n"
"| Black-Scholes equation, basket of 2 assets | 7                      | `BPM-HE` | 0.0085                     | ~4                           | 12.0           |\n"
"| Black-Scholes equation, basket of 3 assets | 9                      | `BPM-HE` | 0.0104                     | ~67                          | 20.7           |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The RMSE is the Root Mean Square Error between the solution and the "
"reference solution (aggregated over the two functions for the Euler, and "
"tensile test cases). For the Burgers, Euler, and tensile test cases, it is "
"measured on the QPU over the sampled points. For the Black-Scholes cases, it"
" is computed inside the domain, excluding the final time and the edges where"
" the solution is imposed, on a fine grid (600 points per axis for 1 asset, "
"80 for 2 assets, 16 for 3 assets), by noiseless simulation at the parameters"
" retained on the QPU. The classical compute time is the time spent by the "
"optimizers on the classical side of the hybrid loop. It is mostly due to the"
" evaluation of the points on the grid, which is easily parallelizable. The "
"QPU time is the quantum processing time billed for the optimization jobs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The number of qubits is lower than in the previous version. The circuit "
"binding feature reduces the QPU time of the runs, not the number of qubits. "
"It remains possible to increase the number of qubits by executing several "
"circuits in parallel, which reduces the QPU time needed even further, and "
"the new version of the function leaves room to scale to larger numbers of "
"qubits and larger problems.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The optimization steps of these runs are described as follows:\n"
"- The `popsize` argument is the number of candidates evaluated at each generation of `CMAES`.\n"
"- The `CMAES` stages can stop early when the cost stagnates.\n"
"- A cost evaluation is the computation of the cost function for one set of circuit parameters, measured on the QPU with the number of shots of the stage.\n"
"- A `CMAES` generation is made of `popsize` evaluations, so the iterations of `CMAES` are counted in generations.\n"
"- An `LS-TRF` iteration computes the Jacobian of the cost function at the current point, with the parameter-shift rule (PSR) here, and evaluates the cost function at one or several trial points.\n"
"- The Jacobian requires its own circuits (the parameter-shifted circuits), which are not counted as evaluations. The number of Jacobians is therefore the number of `LS-TRF` iterations, and the number of evaluations is higher than the number of iterations because some trial points are rejected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Example                        | Stage | Method                   | Shots  | `sigma0` | `popsize` | Iterations                                 |\n"
"| ------------------------------ | ----- | ------------------------ | ------ | -------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n"
"| Inviscid Burgers' equation     | 1     | `CMAES`                  | 3,000  | 0.4      | 12        | 40 generations                             |\n"
"|                                | 2     | `CMAES`                  | 15,000 | 0.1      | 12        | 15 generations                             |\n"
"|                                | 3     | `CMAES`                  | 50,000 | 0.04     | 12        | 6 generations                              |\n"
"| Pressureless Euler's equations | 1     | `CMAES`                  | 2,500  | 0.5      | 12        | 12 generations                             |\n"
"|                                | 2     | `CMAES`                  | 4,000  | 0.15     | 12        | 18 generations                             |\n"
"|                                | 3     | `LS-TRF` with `psr=True` | 10,000 | n/a      | n/a       | 30 evaluations, 7 iterations (7 Jacobians) |\n"
"| Hypoelastic 1D tensile test    | 1     | `CMAES`                  | 2,500  | 0.5      | 12        | 12 generations                             |\n"
"|                                | 2     | `CMAES`                  | 4,000  | 0.15     | 12        | 18 generations                             |\n"
"|                                | 3     | `CMAES`                  | 6,000  | 0.07     | 12        | 12 generations                             |\n"
"|                                | 4     | `LS-TRF` with `psr=True` | 10,000 | n/a      | n/a       | 31 evaluations, 3 iterations (3 Jacobians) |\n"
msgstr ""

